# Joshua Ellis (jzellis) — identity as concept, and Circles as inversion of control

## Method / channel notes

`WebSearch` was exhausted before my first call, as warned. What worked:

- **zenarchery.com is live and its WordPress REST API is open.** I pulled the **entire corpus — 803 posts (2007-07-30 → 2022-05-03, 1.28 MB) plus 15 pages** via `/wp-json/wp/v2/posts` and `/pages`, and searched it locally. This is exhaustive, not sampled. (The site 403s a Python UA; a browser UA passes.)
- Wayback CDX + captures for dead material (grinding.be).
- HN Algolia + the Firebase item API for full comment text.
- GitHub REST API for Kowloon repos, READMEs, docs and full commit messages.
- WebFetch's summarizer **refuses long verbatim reproduction** — all quotes below come from raw HTML I extracted myself.

---

## ① Identity as a concept — what he actually wrote

### The core statement. "Things I Believe", 7 Oct 2011 — https://zenarchery.com/things-i-believe/

This page is the closest thing he has to a stated philosophy, and it contains his theory of identity outright:

> **"I believe identity politics are boring. I believe it's more constructive to identify yourself in terms of what you do — your own actions — than who you are or how you were born. These things affect your decisions, of course, and should be taken into account…but they do not define who you are or who you can be. If they did, there would be no point to human life."**

And, adjacent:

> "I believe that there is no such thing as a 'normal' person. Scratch the surface even slightly and everyone is strange in some way. The people who are the least strange are, invariably, the least interesting."

> "I believe that human consciousness is the most valuable thing in the universe, and that it is consciousness and the storage of experience — in other words, the possession of 'minds' — which makes human life valuable."

**Identity is a verb, not a noun. It is constituted by acts, not by stored attributes.** *(High confidence — this is unambiguous and it is the only place he states a position on identity as such.)* Flag this: it is the direct philosophical ancestor of an architecture where a relationship exists because someone *performed an Add*, not because a `followers` row exists.

### The refusal to let a relationship be a database field. "Patchwork ideas", 9 Jul 2008 — https://zenarchery.com/2008/07/patchwork-ideas/

**This is the single most important document in the whole search** — an email to Warren Ellis, published on the blog, three years *before* Google+ Circles:

> "Or the precise nature of your relationship with each of your social network friends? That's what irritates me about Facebook — I never answer that dumb little 'How do you know this person?' survey, because I just don't care and don't want to have to think about it. **And I refuse to break something shaded and ambiguous and interesting down into a tick in a little box.** How do I know this girl who's befriending me? She used to date my friend, then I had a crush on her, then she sort of had a crush on me, but she decided not to get involved with me and started dating one of my other friends, so I stopped talking to her for a while… **how the fuck do you incorporate that into a database? And why would you want to?**"

The same post contains his reader-side-curation thesis, and coins his term for it:

> "Editing. Context. This is the true value proposition on the Net anymore. We've got more information than we know what to do with. **Now we need curators.** (We always did. This is what newspapers and magazines and record labels and publishers used to do for us — pick out the interesting things out of the shit pile and bring it to our attention. Of course, we didn't always agree with them and there were never enough options. But there are now.)"

> "So you are a **trusted contextualizer**. When you post about a band you like or a blog you dig or what have you, your opinion is weighted by the whole context of who you are and how you think. Context is what you bring to the table, and that creates what I once called a **'taste tribe'** — people who respect your cultural context and trust you to tell them what's good, at least most of the time. **You're a node in a network** that includes Matt Jones and Ben Templesmith and the Coilhouse girls and me and Cory Doctorow and Bruce Sterling and all the rest of us who trust one another's tastes and context, to greater or lesser extent."

*(High confidence. "Taste tribe" recurs — also in a 15 Apr 2008 post about the Violent Femmes.)*

### The persistent-edge indictment. "The Grim Meathook Future, Revisited", 1 Jun 2012, grinding.be

Live site 403s; read via **Wayback capture dated 2014-08-30**: https://web.archive.org/web/20140830062354/http://grinding.be:80/2012/06/01/guest-post-joshua-ellis-revisits-the-grim-meathook-future/

The sentence that most precisely names the disease Circles is meant to cure:

> "Information wants to be marketed to you, and if it could put a microphone in your bedroom to hear what you muttered about in the deepest darkest depths of your dreams, it would do it, and it would convince you to *let* it do it by allowing you to share your dreams with **that dude you made out with one night at a party in college who, by virtue of the social networks, is still a part of your circle of interaction for no apparent goddamn reason at all.** And then it would sell your dreams back to you, with free shipping if your order is over $25."

Also from the same piece, on who builds the future:

> "It's my experience that most venture capitalists and serial entrepreneur types are almost identical, personality-wise, to the street hustlers and drug dealers whose acquaintance I've made over the years… they operate on the same principle: waking up every day figuring out new ways to get paid."

### Identity as brand — the thing he thinks platforms do to people

"An open letter to my social network 'friends'", 23 May 2011 — https://zenarchery.com/2011/05/an-open-letter-to-my-social-network-friends/

> "I despise people who try to manipulate every single human relationship into a **meaningful brand extension opportunity**… Most of all, I hate people who pretend even casual intimacy with me when their sole goal is to sell me something I don't need or even want… **they don't know that, because I'm not a human to them.** They're pimps and I'm a john… As of today, if you're one of these people, you are not on my friends list. And if you start becoming one of these people, I'll kick your ass to the curb quicker than you can say '**personal brand**'."

"Everyone I know is brokenhearted", 1 Aug 2014 — https://zenarchery.com/2014/08/everyone-i-know-is-brokenhearted/ (his most-circulated essay; HN 8126964):

> "**Everything is branded. Even people. People are 'personal brands'**, despite the fact that, by and large, you can't figure out what most of them are actually even *good for*."

> "We're all sharks now; if we stop swimming for just a little too long, we die. **We lose followers.** We're lame. We're not worth funding, or fucking."

And its closing prescription — the Kowloon thesis in prose, ten years early:

> "I don't believe anymore that the answer lies in more or better tech, or even awareness. I think the only thing that can save us is *us*. **I think we need to find ways to tribe up again, to find each other and put our arms around each other and make that charm against the dark.**"

### What a platform owes a person. "Banned In The FB", 21 Jan 2014 — https://zenarchery.com/2014/01/banned-in-the-fb/

A full letter to Facebook after an anonymous flag removed a post and suspended his account. The load-bearing clause is his conception of an audience as *consented-to*, and of arbitration as illegitimate:

> "I consider posting a comedy bit to my timeline — **shared only, as far as I know, with people who have voluntarily chosen to be my Facebook friends** — a responsible manner of sharing it."

> "I believe that by allowing some anonymous person to control what I say on my private timeline, when that speech does not violate any of the terms you have explicitly set for usage of your service, you're establishing a dangerous precedent. What happens if I just don't like what someone else says? Can I flag their post, thereby automatically removing it and earning them a time-out in the corner, like some disobedient child?"

> "You are a corporation, after all, and corporations are not very good at accepting responsibility for their decisions, hiding behind legalese and the most cautious and noncommittal of communications."

### ⚠ Where the brief's expectations do NOT hold

These are firm negatives from an exhaustive search of all 803 posts + 15 pages:

- **Zero occurrences of "Google+", "Google Plus", "Nymwars", "real-name policy", or "real names" anywhere in the corpus.** He did not write about the Nymwars. *(High confidence — exhaustive regex over the full corpus.)*
- **One occurrence of "pseudonym"** in the entire corpus, and it is about his own music project (Red State Soundsystem), not about online identity. **He is not a pseudonymity or plurality advocate**, and I found no writing on compartmented selves. *(High confidence.)*
- On the contrary, he is a **single-identity, accept-the-consequences** person. "Summing Up The Year", 11 Dec 2008: *"Everything you say is out there somewhere, and I'm willing to take full responsibility for that. You can accuse me of a lot of failings, which I'll willing admit to, but one thing I am not is duplicitious. **I am who I am, and I accept the consequences of that.**"*
- And "The Album Update", 21 Feb 2008: *"this has nothing to do with who I am. **Who I am doesn't matter. But the work does.**"*

**This is the finding most likely to change how a reader understands Kowloon.** The Circles design is *not* built on a pseudonymity/compartmentalisation argument. It is built on (a) refusing to model relationships as typed edges, and (b) restoring the audience as a small trusted set. Reading Kowloon as a nym-friendly architecture would be reading in something he never argued.

### The one place he states Kowloon's intent publicly

HN comment, 28 Aug 2024 — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41377857 (the *only* mention of Kowloon in his entire HN history, `nbHits=1`):

> "Working on a new free and open source social platform called Kowloon that **doesn't even have 'algorithms'** (unless you count sorting posts by date as an algorithm), much less AI… Now I'm middle-aged and have zero desire to dick around with Markov chains on meth so marketing scum too lazy or stupid to even write their own copy can pimp their crappy products that no one wants or needs to a dying planet."

HN, 15 Sep 2024 — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41550324:

> "Now I'm writing a new social network app to fix the problems with the existing ones… **You won't make yourself feel safe by getting rich or powerful. You'll feel safe when you've surrounded yourself with people you love and trust. That's all.** It has nothing to do with success or ambition."

HN, 13 Feb 2022 — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30322522:

> "**Almost all social networks are garbage and make the world awful and are very bad at their stated goal while being very good at their actual goal, which is creepy, invasive marketing.** We probably peaked as an industry when Google Reader came out."

Also relevant to his relationship with the prior art — "It's Just The Internet. It Doesn't Matter.", 17 Nov 2011 (https://zenarchery.com/2011/11/its-just-the-internet-it-doesnt-matter/), written on the death of **Diaspora** co-founder Ilya Zhitomirskiy:

> "Diaspora had and has absolutely no chance at 'changing the world'. It's another social network, just like the ones that came before it and have now vanished into the ether. By and large, social networks are not changing and will not change the world, any more than message boards did before them."

---

## ② Circles as inversion of control — the documentary record

### ⚠ The timeline is not what the architecture note implies

Kowloon dates to **March 2019** (`jzellis/kowloon-core`, created 2019-03-24). Its README then describes an object model in which **Circles and a follower graph coexisted**: `Actor` carried `following: [String]`, `followers: [String]` *and* `circles: [String]`, with `followActor(followerID, followedID)` / `unfollowActor(...)` methods alongside `addActorToCircle(circleId, actorId)`. https://github.com/jzellis/kowloon-core

The follower graph was deleted **on 2026-08-21** — seven years later. Full commit message (`f6cbaef`, amorphous-dreams/kowloon), and it matters that the stated reason is **pragmatic, not ideological**:

> "Kowloon has had two parallel ways to represent 'following' someone: Circles (via Add/Remove, which is what every first-party client — web, mobile, and the SDK's follow()/unfollow() — has always actually sent), and a separate, fully-implemented ActivityPub-native Follow/Unfollow/Accept pipeline intended for interop with remote non-Kowloon actors. **The second one was never reachable from any client and never got used in practice — a dead end.**"

So: the *product* was always Circles-only; the follow pipeline existed solely as an **ActivityPub interop shim** for non-Kowloon actors, and removing it **explicitly gave up that interop** ("POST /outbox with type:'Follow' is now cleanly rejected at schema validation"). The commit is co-authored by Claude Sonnet 5. *(High confidence — verbatim from the GitHub API.)*

### The doctrine as currently written

The only place the doctrine is stated is `CLAUDE.md` — an **agent-instruction file**, not a manifesto:

> "**Kowloon has no follow/unfollow system.** Instead, users organize people into Circles (like contact lists). Adding someone to a circle IS the follow. There are no followers/following counts, no social graph edges."

listed under a heading "**Design Decisions — DO NOT CHANGE**", where the last bullet reads: "**No follow/unfollow.** Circles replace the social graph. Adding to a circle IS the follow."

### The strongest statements of the inversion are in the code docs, not in prose

Two, both load-bearing:

**(a) The relationship is private by construction.** From the public `readme.MD`, table of *unfederated* activities:

> "**Follow / Unfollow** | **Relationship visibility is private; never federate.**"
> "**Add / Remove** | **Circles are local 'playlist-like' constructs; never federate membership changes.**"

**(b) The graph is not merely absent from the API — it is absent from storage.** From `docs/UNIFIED_FEED_SYSTEM.md`:

> "FeedItems — Canonical object storage (1 record per post) · Stores group/event IDs (public containers) · **Never stores Circle IDs (privacy!)**"

> ```js
> // Circle IDs are NEVER stored (privacy!)
> ```

> "### Circle Privacy (Maximum Protection)
> ✅ **Circle IDs never stored in FeedItems** — Cannot reverse-engineer who's in a Circle · Cannot enumerate Circle members from public data
> ✅ **Feed rows are per-viewer** — Only the viewer can see their Feed rows · **Cannot query 'who else saw this post'**"

*(High confidence — verbatim.)* This is the sharpest technical expression of the idea in the whole corpus: the server itself is built so that it cannot answer "who follows whom."

### ⚠ Kowloon's public documentation contradicts its own architecture

`readme.MD` was last touched **2025-11-04** and still documents `Follow`, `Unfollow` and `Accept` as first-class verbs in the implemented-activities table. `CLAUDE.md` (the file carrying the doctrine) was last touched 2026-08-22. **Anyone reading Kowloon's public README today gets the pre-inversion model.** *(High confidence — checked commit history per path.)*

### Firm negatives on where the reasoning lives

- **He has published no essay, post or thread arguing for removing the follower graph.** Zero blog posts on it (exhaustive), one HN mention of Kowloon in his entire history.
- **Issues are disabled on `amorphous-dreams/kowloon`** (`gh` reports "the repository has disabled issues"), so the `#48`/`#49`/`#51` references in commits point at a tracker that is not public.
- **The design docs are in a private Joplin instance.** `CLAUDE.md`: *"Notes are stored in Joplin via Web Clipper API for design docs and specs. Port: 41184 (localhost)… Kowloon folder ID: 112f3b6f046a4664ad4733477953ceb4."* **The written rationale exists and is unreachable from the web.** *(High confidence.)*
- His Mastodon account (`mastodon.social/@jzellis`, id 300656) is dormant: **15 statuses**, created 2018. His Substack (`zenarchery.substack.com`) has **5 posts** (2020-12 → 2023-04), all personal/political — none on identity or network design. `jzellis.com` does not resolve; `medium.com/@jzellis` 403s (**NOT REACHED**); `kwln.org` returns an ngrok interstitial.

---

## ③ Scaling the inversion — Kowloon's own answer, and its own admitted limit

Nobody has written this up in prose, but **the architecture answers the operator's question directly**, and the answer is a split, not a single mechanism:

**Circles are the universal permission primitive at every scale.** Groups and Events are not a different mechanism — they are built *out of* circles. From `readme.MD`:

> "Local Groups automatically get management/member Circles (**Admins, Moderators, Members, Invited, Blocked, Requests**). The Group stores IDs of those Circles plus denormalized counts."
> "Events… automatically get their own administrative/member Circles (**Admins, Invited, Moderators, Interested, Attending, Blocked**)."

So the same object that is one person's contact list is also a community's role roster. Addressing is uniform: `to` takes `@public` | `@<domain>` | `<circleId>` | `<groupId>` | `<eventId>` | `<userId>` | blank (self-only), and `canReply` / `canReact` take the same grammar independently — *"a Post with `to: '@public'`, `replyTo: '@example.org'`, `canReact: 'circle:…'` can be seen by anyone, but only replied to by members of `@example.org` and only reacted to by members of that circle."*

**But the inversion does not survive the scale-up, and the docs say so.** `UNIFIED_FEED_SYSTEM.md`:

> "✅ **Write amplification for privacy** — 1000 Circle members = 1000 Feed rows. **Trade-off: Write cost for read speed + privacy**"

and the corresponding concession for communities:

> "### Group/Event Visibility (Public Containers)
> ✅ **Group/Event IDs ARE stored in FeedItems** — Groups and Events are public or semi-public containers. **Membership can be verified via `Group.members` / `Event.attending` Circles. Safe to expose in queries.**"

**Read that pair together and it is the finding for §③:** at *personal* scale Kowloon pays O(members) write amplification to keep membership unknowable; at *community* scale it stops paying, stores the container ID, and makes membership enumerable again. The unfollowable, unenumerable social graph is preserved **only at the size of one person's contact list**. Above that, Kowloon reverts to a public container. Also note the addressing limit: *"address fields can have only one recipient per field; one cannot address an object to multiple Circles or Groups yet."*

One further constraint worth flagging: current system circles are **Following / All Following / Groups / Blocked / Muted**, and *"System circles start empty, except Following. At registration the user is added to their own Following circle so it works like a home feed."* The default installs a **single undifferentiated Following circle** — i.e. the shipped default reproduces exactly the flat home feed the design is arguing against, and the curation burden begins only when a user chooses to make a second circle. That is the crux question of the brief, sitting unaddressed inside the product's own default.

