# Addendum 3 — ③ how far up the inversion scales

## The convergent result

Three unrelated systems, three independent measurements, same number:

| System | Users who ever left the default | Source |
|---|---|---|
| **Bluesky custom feeds** | **2.8%** ever liked one | arXiv 2405.17571 |
| **Bluesky starter packs** | **4.37%** ever used follow-all | (agent) |
| **Zulip home view** | **1.2%** ever changed it | Zulip Year in Review 2024 |

I verified the Bluesky figure myself from the paper's full text rather than its abstract — https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.17571v3, dataset of 5M users, Feb 2023 – May 2024:

> "In total, **39,639 feeds have been created by 18,352 active users**… In our dataset, **139,033 Users have used this feature and liked feeds 295,902 times.**"

> "On average, **feeds only attracted two likes — showing that most feeds receive little to no engagement.** Conversely, users who actively liked feeds liked, on average, over fourteen distinct feeds. We also see that **a large number of feeds was created by a small minority of highly active users.**"

> "The creation of feeds has been taken up by users with enthusiasm with almost forty thousand choices present for users. **However, only a small minority of users have liked a feed.**"

139,033 / 5,000,000 = **2.8%**. Power-law exponent α = 3.41 on likes-per-feed; mean 2.13, max 1,799. *(High confidence — read from the PDF directly.)*

**And among the minority who do choose, it re-concentrates:**

| Concentration | Figure |
|---|---|
| Starter packs: top 20% → | **97.17%** of induced edges |
| Feed generators: top 3 hosts → | **95.8%** of all generators |
| Mastodon: top 5% of instances → | **90.6%** of users (2019) |
| Mastodon: single largest instance → | **23.5%** of the fediverse (agent's own FediDB measurement, 2026-09-04) |

**This is the direct answer to the operator's live question, and it is not encouraging for the naive version of the inversion.** Give readers full control and ~97% take the default; the ~3% who curate collapse onto a handful of hosts and packs. The follower graph is not what produces concentration — concentration survives its removal.

## ⚠ The mechanism is not the one usually blamed

The agent's most useful correction: **it is not choice overload.** The meta-analytic effect of choice overload is **D = 0.02 across 63 conditions — indistinguishable from zero.** The operative effect is the **default effect (d = 0.68), acting strongest through endowment** — a default reads as the status quo, and the status quo is what people keep.

Design consequence, stated as evidence not advice: what determines uptake is *what ships as the default*, not how easy curation is made. Google+ made Circles maximally visible and got "moderate use" (my §② addendum); Bluesky made feeds a marketplace and got 2.8%. **The lever is the default, and Kowloon's default is a single undifferentiated `Following` circle** — the flat home feed the design argues against.

## The two hard architectural constraints, from the people who hit them

**1986, LISTSERV** — the pre-automation model was literally a human curating the subscription set. https://www.lsoft.com/products/listserv-history.asp:

> "To join a mailing list, you would write to INFO@BITNIC (which… **was a human being**) and ask to be added to the list."
> "**Hundreds of requests were received by INFO@BITNIC every day, and it could take days or even weeks to be added**… **the traffic from the mailing lists alone was threatening to make private email totally unusable, with delivery delays approaching a week.**"
> "**INFO@BITNIC was now manned full-time by someone who did nothing but add and remove people from lists all day long**, and they were still unable to meet demand."

The fix was **peering** — replicating the audience set geographically so fan-out crossed the expensive link once. **Not scaling the curator; replicating the set.**

**2021, Matrix** — Matthew Hodgson, project lead, https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25977828:

> "**the resource requirements depend on the complexity of the rooms its users participate in, not the number of users on the server.** So a single user server can use serious RAM if that one user goes and joins a bunch of busy rooms with hundreds of thousands of users in them… **we haven't figured out a good way of solving it.**"

> "**if you want to participate in a room whose users are spread over 1000 servers, you'll need a server beefy enough to talk to 1000 servers every time someone says something.**" (https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=34289826)

**In a federated design the cost of belonging accrues to the participant's node and scales with the set's decentralisation. Joining is the expensive act.** *Flag this hard against Kowloon:* its own docs already price this — *"1000 Circle members = 1000 Feed rows. Trade-off: Write cost for read speed + privacy"* — but that is the **sender**-side cost. Matrix's finding is that in federation the **receiver**-side cost is the one that bites, and Kowloon's batch-pull federation puts exactly that load on the pulling server.

## The governance result, written down in defeat

Big-8 Management FAQ, **Wayback capture 2015-01-18** — https://web.archive.org/web/20150118063107/http://www.big-8.org:80/articles/b/i/g/Big-8_Management_FAQ.html:

> "**Will the board make the people in our newsgroup obey the charter of the group? — No.**
> • There are too many newsgroups… for the board to become involved.
> • **There is no way to identify who 'the people in our newsgroup' are.**
> • There is no mechanism in NNTP to enforce punishments…
> • Except for moderated groups… **newsgroups have no 'owners'.**"

**A flood-fill transport cannot hold a curated audience; it can only hold a topic name and hope.** The ~100-vote plebiscite ran ~1987–2005 and was replaced by an appointed board on 2006-09-30; the current process has no public vote threshold at all.

Also: **the Usenet "collapse" is misread.** Daily volume went 12 GiB (1998) → 274 TiB (Feb 2024), but Big-8 *text* stayed ~25,000 messages/day, and binaries are "over 99 percent of article data." **The discussion layer stayed roughly flat while the carrier grew ~60,000× in bytes.** Post-1994 growth is freight, not community.

## Zulip — the one system that states the asymmetry as its bet

tabbott (Tim Abbott), https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=19285211:

> "You can argue it's slightly more work for the person sending a message to type a topic… **Whereas what Zulip's model saves your organization is every one of the N users who can have to read the conversation needing to spend much more time and mental effort.**"

**O(1) sender cost buys O(N) reader saving** — the explicit justification for making the sender curate. And from https://zulip.com/why-zulip/: *"channels determine who gets a message. Each conversation within a channel is labeled with a topic"* — **recipient selection and conversation grouping as orthogonal axes**, which is the primitive Kowloon is reaching for with independent `to` / `canReply` / `canReact`.

But the counter-datum from Zulip's own 2024 review is the sharpest thing in the whole brief: **"@topic mentions are used 9x less often than @all mentions, and 21x less often than personal mentions."** *The narrowest addressing verb is the least used, by ~9× against the broadest* — in the one product built entirely around narrow addressing, staffed by people who chose it.

## Genuinely unmeasured — treat as open

- **No study anywhere measures a ceiling on concurrent group memberships.** Three independent query framings returned nothing. The Reddit proxy suggests **~2–5 concurrent with ~40% monthly churn** — an order of magnitude below the ~150 dyadic Dunbar figure. *(This is the number the operator's §③ question most wants and it does not exist.)*
- **No sourced spam-share-of-Usenet figure exists** for any year. Treat "X% of Usenet was spam" as unsourced.
- **Bluesky's own transparency reports publish extensive moderation volume and nothing at all on algorithmic-choice uptake.** The agent flags this as the most telling negative result in the brief, and I agree: the platform making the strongest public case for algorithmic choice does not report whether anyone uses it.

Attention ceiling, for completeness: per-source responsiveness decays as **~nf^−0.79** (Hodas & Lerman); aggregate attention is conserved. A reader curating 200 sources gets one reader's worth of attention, divided.

## Status of the remaining strand

The **§② lineage agent** (Diaspora aspects, Cohost, Path, Twitter lists, Nostr, Paul Adams, Stone Temple) has still not reported and I know nothing of its results. I covered its crux myself in Addendum 2 — the three Circles papers, the Facebook 5%/10%/17% friend-list baseline, and the finding that **Google+'s death is not a Circles post-mortem**. The remaining items stay **NOT REACHED**.

Confidence overall: **medium-high**. The load-bearing weakness is the Bluesky 2.8% — single paper, single platform, early-2024, and "liked a feed" is a public proxy for a *private* bookmark action nobody outside Bluesky can observe.
